            <ArticleTitle>Longstanding insulin dependent diabetics may not require insulin after the introduction of GLP-1 analogues.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2048004015576008</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/2048004015576008</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Glucagon like peptide (GLP-1) analogues are a relatively novel medication developed primarily for the treatment of type 2 diabetes since 2005. Although GLP-1 analogues have been shown to be more effective in the first few years of diagnosis in type 2 diabetes, we report a case of a patient with longstanding insulin-dependent diabetes started on a GLP-1 analogue, liraglutide, who now has controlled blood sugars without the need of insulin.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
